.\" Manual page for ricer. Kept in step with the command line by tests/test_manpage.py.
.TH RICER 1 "2026-10-05" "ricer 0.4.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ricer \- compose and apply a different GNOME desktop look on every run
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ricer instant
[\fB\-\-cool\fR \fIN\fR]
[\fB\-\-ease\fR \fIN\fR]
[\fB\-\-warmth\fR \fIN\fR]
[\fB\-\-chaos\fR \fIN\fR]
[\fB\-\-all\fR \fIN\fR]
[\fB\-\-seed\fR \fIN\fR]
[\fB\-\-keep\fR \fIPARTS\fR]
[\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
[\fB\-\-json\fR]
.br
.B ricer reroll
[\fIthe same options\fR]
.br
.B ricer default
[\fBset\fR | \fBshow\fR | \fBclear\fR]
.br
.B ricer revert
[\fB\-\-all\fR]
.br
.B ricer status
.br
.B ricer setup
.br
.B ricer wallpapers
\fBlist\fR | \fBadd\fR \fIFILE\fR... | \fBfetch\fR [\fB\-\-count\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-query\fR \fIWORDS\fR] | \fBauto\fR [\fBon\fR | \fBoff\fR] [\fB\-\-cap\fR \fIN\fR]
.br
.B ricer widgets
\fBstart\fR | \fBstop\fR
.br
.B ricer help
[\fICOMMAND\fR [\fIACTION\fR]]
.br
.B ricer \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ricer
composes a whole desktop look for GNOME and applies it at once, with no logout:
wallpaper, colours, top bar, dock, terminal, app windows, widgets on the desktop and
widgets inside the top bar.
.PP
Four dials steer it. They do not select a look; they tilt the odds of what a run
produces. Running the same command again gives another wallpaper and other widgets,
in other designs and other places. Every run prints a
.IR seed ,
and the same dials with that seed give the same look again.
.PP
.B ricer
records the previous value of everything it changes, so a look can be undone
exactly. See
.B ricer revert
below.
.SH THE DIALS
Each dial takes a whole number from 1 to 10. A dial that is left out is 5 for
.BR "ricer instant" ,
and keeps its current value for
.BR "ricer reroll" .
.TP
\fB\-\-cool\fR \fIN\fR
Flashiness, from calm and minimal (1) to loud and flashy (10). Higher values make
more widgets on the desktop likely (about
.I N
minus two), along with bolder designs, ornaments, saturated colour, gradients,
blur and a flashier top bar.
.TP
\fB\-\-ease\fR \fIN\fR
Practicality, from looks first (1) to usability first (10). Higher values put
information into the top bar (system stats from about 5, a media player from
about 7), and favour larger text, more opaque cards, calmer wallpapers and tidy
column layouts.
.TP
\fB\-\-warmth\fR \fIN\fR
Mood, from cold blues (1) to warm ambers (10). Higher values favour warm wallpapers
and hues, round shapes, serif type, an analogue clock and a greeting. Lower values
favour sharp outlined cards, mono and condensed type, a digital clock and system
stats.
.TP
\fB\-\-chaos\fR \fIN\fR
How far a run may stray from what the other three dials suggest. At 1 a run stays
close to their favourites, in wallpapers as in everything else. At 10 nearly
anything in the vocabulary can come up.
.TP
\fB\-\-all\fR \fIN\fR
Set cool, ease and warmth to
.I N
together. A single dial given as well overrides it. Chaos is not affected.
.PP
Some things are rules and not odds. Text stays readable: wherever the wallpaper
under a widget is busy or bright, the widget gets a card behind it, and large
display text at least a dark outline. Widgets do not overlap one another and keep
clear of the dock. From ease 7 up the dock never hides. At cool 1 and 2 there is
at most one widget on the desktop.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS ricer instant
Compose a new look from the dials and apply it. Prints the look, any feature that
was skipped because this desktop lacks it, and the command that brings the same
look back.
.TP
\fB\-\-seed\fR \fIN\fR
Use this seed and draw none. With the same dials, the same wallpaper folder and
the same screen size, this gives the same look. Without it,
.B ricer
passes over seeds that lead to a wallpaper used in the last three looks.
.TP
\fB\-\-keep\fR \fIPARTS\fR
Carry parts of the current look over and draw the rest anew.
.I PARTS
is a comma\-separated list of
.BR wallpaper ,
.B style
(which includes the colours),
.BR widgets ,
.BR layout ,
.B bar
and
.BR dock .
.B layout
alone keeps the layout template; together with
.B widgets
it also keeps every widget in its place. Needs a look to be applied already.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print the look and what would be skipped, and change nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the look in full as JSON.
.SS ricer reroll
The same as
.BR "ricer instant" ,
except that dials you leave out keep the values of the current look. It takes the
same options. Fails if no look is applied.
.SS ricer default
With no action, apply your default look.
.TP
.B set
Save the look that is applied now as the default.
.TP
.B show
Print the default look.
.TP
.B clear
Forget the default look.
.PP
The default is stored as the complete look and not as dials and a seed, so it does
not change when wallpapers are added or
.B ricer
is updated. It only needs its own wallpaper file to still exist.
.SS ricer revert
Undo the last look: every setting and file goes back to what it was before that
look was applied, and the look before it becomes the current one again.
.TP
.B \-\-all
Undo everything
.B ricer
has ever changed, restoring each setting to the value it had before
.B ricer
first touched it. The history of recent looks is forgotten. The default look is
kept.
.SS ricer status
Print the current look, whether a default look is saved, which optional features
this desktop supports, and how many wallpapers the library holds.
.SS ricer setup
For each optional GNOME Shell extension that is not installed (User Themes, Blur
my Shell, Vitals, Media Controls), ask GNOME Shell to install it from
extensions.gnome.org. GNOME shows its own dialog for each one, and nothing is
installed unless you confirm it there. If GNOME has all user extensions switched
off, they are switched back on, and User Themes and Blur my Shell are enabled if
they were installed but switched off. The current look is then applied again so
that it uses what has become available.
.SS ricer wallpapers
.TP
.B list
List every wallpaper with its measured warmth (\-1 cold to +1 warm), colour,
detail and brightness (each 0 to 1).
.TP
\fBadd\fR \fIFILE\fR...
Copy images (jpg, jpeg, png or webp) into the library.
.TP
\fBfetch\fR [\fB\-\-count\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-query\fR \fIWORDS\fR]
Download up to
.I N
new images (12 if not given) from wallhaven.cc. With
.IR WORDS ,
the most favourited images that match them. Without, a mix: a few from each of
several themes (landscape, sunset, night city, sky, forest, rain, ocean,
mountains, stars, street, cyberpunk, anime scenery), drawn from each theme's most
favourited. Only images from its general and anime categories that are rated safe
for work are requested, in 16:9 and at least 1920x1080. Images the library has, or
has held before, are skipped. The images belong to their artists.
.TP
\fBauto\fR [\fBon\fR | \fBoff\fR] [\fB\-\-cap\fR \fIN\fR]
With nothing, show the setting. While it is
.B on
(as it is until switched off), every look applied by
.B ricer instant
or
.B ricer reroll
fetches 3 new images in the background, as
.B fetch
without words does; they can come up from the next look on. The switch itself
does not wait for them.
.B \-\-cap
sets how many images the library holds (200 if never set). Once it holds more,
the oldest fetched images are deleted to make room. Images you added yourself are
never deleted, nor are the wallpapers of the current look, the look
.B ricer revert
returns to, the last few looks and the default look.
.SS ricer widgets
\fBstart\fR or \fBstop\fR the process that draws the desktop widgets.
.B ricer instant
starts and stops it as needed; these are for troubleshooting.
.SS ricer help
With no argument, print commands to try, the dials and a one\-line summary of
every command. With a command, and for
.B wallpapers
an action, print its options.
.SH WHAT A LOOK IS MADE OF
.TP
.B Style
One style is shared by every part of a look: corner radius, how opaque cards are
(down to no card at all), their border, type weight, typeface (interface,
condensed, mono, serif or geometric, using fonts that are installed), capitals
for labels, flat or gradient accents, and text scale. The accent colours come
from the wallpaper.
.TP
.B Desktop widgets
A clock (one line of digits, stacked digits, an analogue dial, or the time in
words), a greeting, a calendar (month, week or a single day), a media player
(card, pill or cover art) for any MPRIS player, system readings (bars, rings or
one line), progress through the day, week, month and year, and an ornament
drawn from the seed. Each is placed in one of nine zones of the screen, where
the wallpaper is calm and its subject stays uncovered.
.TP
.B Top bar
Left as it is, plain and transparent, one floating piece, three floating cards,
or solid. System stats and a media player can sit in the bar itself.
.TP
.B Windows
App windows take the colour of the look's cards, its accent, and the outline its
cards have. GNOME Terminal becomes one pane of tinted glass, title bar included;
it is more see\-through where the look's cards are fainter and less over a bright
wallpaper, so that text stays readable.
.TP
.B The rest
The dock's side, hiding, size, indicator and tint; the icon and cursor set.
While desktop widgets are shown, the Home and drive icons
on the desktop are hidden.
.SH EXAMPLES
A new look with every dial at 5:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
ricer instant
.EE
.RE
.PP
Loud and cold; then calm; then anything goes:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
ricer instant \-\-cool 9 \-\-warmth 2
ricer instant \-\-all 3
ricer instant \-\-all 8 \-\-chaos 10
.EE
.RE
.PP
See what a run would give without applying it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
ricer instant \-\-cool 7 \-\-dry\-run
.EE
.RE
.PP
Another look with the same dials; then keep the wallpaper and colours and change
the rest; then the same but a little warmer:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
ricer reroll
ricer reroll \-\-keep wallpaper,style
ricer reroll \-\-keep wallpaper \-\-warmth 8
.EE
.RE
.PP
Bring an earlier look back from the line it printed:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
ricer instant \-\-cool 7 \-\-ease 6 \-\-warmth 6 \-\-chaos 5 \-\-seed 1969942044
.EE
.RE
.PP
Keep the look you have as your default, try others, and return to it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
ricer default set
ricer instant \-\-all 9
ricer default
.EE
.RE
.PP
Undo the last look; undo everything:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
ricer revert
ricer revert \-\-all
.EE
.RE
.PP
Give the dials more to choose from:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
ricer wallpapers fetch \-\-count 20
ricer wallpapers fetch \-\-query "anime sunset landscape"
ricer wallpapers auto off
ricer wallpapers auto on \-\-cap 300
ricer wallpapers list
.EE
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I \(ti/Pictures/Wallpapers
The wallpaper library.
.TP
.I \(ti/.config/ricer/state.json
The current look, the recent looks, and what is needed to undo them.
.TP
.I \(ti/.config/ricer/default.json
The default look.
.TP
.I \(ti/.config/ricer/widgets.json
What the widget process draws. Rewritten by every look; the process follows it.
.TP
.I \(ti/.config/ricer/wallpapers.json
What
.B ricer wallpapers auto
was set to.
.TP
.I \(ti/.cache/ricer/
The analysis of each wallpaper, the widget process's log, which images were
ever fetched (fetched.json) and what each background fetch did (topup.log).
.TP
.I \(ti/.local/share/themes/Ricer/
The generated top\-bar theme.
.TP
.I \(ti/.local/share/themes/
Besides the folder above: a copy of the stock app theme's name (two folders, such
as Yaru\-purple\-dark and Yaru\-purple) whose files hand over to the recoloured
stylesheet. Present only while a look is applied.
.TP
.I \(ti/.local/share/ricer/sheets/
The recoloured app stylesheets, each named after its content.
.TP
.I \(ti/.config/gtk\-4.0/gtk.css
Your own file. Between its two marker comments
.B ricer
keeps the look's colours for libadwaita apps; the rest of the file is not touched.
.TP
.I \(ti/.config/autostart/ricer\-widgets.desktop
Starts the widgets at login. Present only while the look has desktop widgets.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B RICER_WALLPAPERS
Use this folder as the wallpaper library.
.TP
.BR XDG_CONFIG_HOME ", " XDG_CACHE_HOME ", " XDG_DATA_HOME
Where the files above are kept, if set.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success. This includes having nothing to revert.
.TP
.B 1
What was asked could not be done: no wallpapers, no look to reroll or keep parts
of, no default look, a download or an apply that failed.
.TP
.B 2
The command line was wrong, the desktop is not GNOME, or the desktop could not
be reached.
.SH NOTES
Desktop widgets need an X11 session; GNOME on Wayland does not let a program pin
a window to the desktop. There, everything else is applied, including the
widgets in the top bar.
.PP
Features that depend on something this desktop lacks are skipped, and
.B ricer
says which. The top bar's style needs the User Themes extension, stats in the
bar need Vitals, a player in the bar needs Media Controls, and blur needs Blur
my Shell.
.B ricer setup
installs them.
.PP
Window colours need the Yaru app theme. GNOME Terminal and GTK 3 apps change while
they run. Apps built on libadwaita, such as Files and Settings, read colours when
they start and so change when next opened. Sandboxed apps and apps that draw
their own windows keep their look. If you have your own copy of the theme in
.IR \(ti/.local/share/themes ,
.B ricer
leaves it alone and says so.
.PP
Placement keeps widgets off the part of a wallpaper that stands out. That is
judged from colour, brightness and detail, and is not always what a person would
call the subject.
.PP
The word clock and the greeting are in English only.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gnome\-extensions (1),
.BR gsettings (1),
.BR dconf (1)
.PP
Source and issues: https://github.com/walkingkindle/instant\-ricer
